course: physics
lesson: 10
title: Swings and pendulums
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A swing is a pendulum: a weight hanging from a point, going back and forth with its own steady rhythm.
explain: Its rhythm is set by the length of the chains, not by how hard you are pushed or how heavy you are, which is why an old grandfather clock can count time with a swinging pendulum.
explain: On the way down a swing turns height into speed, and on the way up it turns speed back into height, while the air and creaky chains slowly steal a little each time.
explain: Pushes that come in time with the rhythm add up: that is how you pump yourself higher, and why marching soldiers break step on a bridge.
fact: After one push a swing goes back and forth lower and lower until it stops, because air and the creaking chains take away its energy.
fact: Moving your legs and leaning at the right moments adds a little energy each swing; kicking at the wrong moments slows you down.
fact: For swings that are not too big, a big push and a small push give almost the same time for one back-and-forth.
fact: Galileo noticed this watching a swinging lamp in a cathedral, timing it with his pulse.
fact: A short pendulum swings quickly and a long one slowly; one four times as long takes twice as long to swing.
fact: A heavy child and a light child on the same swing take the same time to go back and forth.
fact: A grandfather clock counts the swings of its pendulum, and clockmakers move a little weight up or down to make it run faster or slower: moving it up makes the pendulum shorter, so the clock runs faster.
fact: At the top of a swing you stop for an instant and feel almost weightless; at the bottom you feel heaviest, because the swing pulls you round the curve.
fact: A swing is fastest at the bottom and slowest at the top, and a roller coaster swaps height and speed the same way.
fact: Pushes that match something's own rhythm build up; a London footbridge wobbled on its first day in 2000 because walkers fell into step with its sway.
fact: A wrecking ball gets its power from its height and weight, and it can swing back, so the area is cleared of people.
fact: In Newton's cradle, swing one ball in and one flies out the other side, swing two in and two fly out, because the knock passes through the still balls.
fact: A wine glass sings when you rub its rim with a wet finger, because the finger sticks and slips in time with the glass's own wobble; more water makes the note lower.
exercise: Why does a swing slowly stop after one push? | Air and the creaking chains take away its energy | Gravity gets stronger and stronger each time it swings back | The swing gets heavier each time | The seat runs out of push inside
exercise: How do you swing higher by pumping your legs? | You move at the right moments and add energy each swing | You make yourself heavier | You push the air backwards hard | You make the chains shorter
exercise: What happens if you kick at the wrong moments? | You slow yourself down | You go higher faster | Nothing at all changes | The swing stops at once
exercise: Does a higher swing take much longer to come back? | No, the time stays almost the same | Yes, it takes twice as long to come back | Yes, a lot longer | No, it comes back twice as fast
exercise: Who first noticed this by watching a swinging lamp in a cathedral? | Galileo | Isaac Newton | Archimedes | Leonardo the painter
exercise: How could you make a pendulum swing more slowly? | Make its string longer | Make its weight heavier | Push it much harder | Make its string shorter
exercise: Would a grown-up on the swing go back and forth more slowly than a child? | No, the weight does not change the rhythm | Yes, heavier people always swing much more slowly | Yes, much more slowly | No, the grown-up goes faster
exercise: What sets the rhythm of a swing? | The length of the chains | The weight of whoever is riding it | How hard you are pushed each time | The colour the seat is painted
exercise: Why can a grandfather clock keep good time? | Its pendulum's rhythm depends only on its length | Its pendulum never slows down at all | It is wound up every second | Its weight changes every hour
exercise: How do clockmakers make a pendulum clock faster or slower? | They move a little weight up or down | They paint the pendulum a new colour | They push the pendulum harder | They add oil to the face
exercise: Where on the swing do you feel heaviest? | At the bottom | At the very top | Halfway up | You feel the same everywhere
exercise: Where is the swing fastest? | At the bottom | At the very top | Just after the top | It is the same speed everywhere
exercise: Why don't soldiers march in step over a bridge? | Their rhythm could make the bridge shake more and more | Bridges are too narrow for marching | Marching wears out the bridge paint | The noise scares the fish below
exercise: Why did a London footbridge wobble on its first day? | Walkers fell into step with its sway | The wind blew hard against it from two sides at once | It was built from soft wood | Too many dogs were walking on it
exercise: Where does a wrecking ball get its power? | From its weight and the height it swings from | From a strong engine hidden inside the heavy ball | From magnets in the building | From the wind around the crane
exercise: In Newton's cradle, why do the middle balls stay still? | The knock passes through them to the last ball | They are glued together | They are heavier than the end balls | They are not touching each other
exercise: Why does a wine glass sing when you rub its rim with a wet finger? | Your finger pushes it in time with its own wobble | The water inside starts to boil from the warmth of your finger | The glass is full of tiny bells | The air inside is squeezed out
test: My mate says if you push the swing harder it'll go back and forth way faster. right? | No, it goes higher but takes about the same time | Yes, twice as hard means twice as fast | Yes, hard pushes speed up the rhythm | No, it takes much longer
test: What would happen to a swing in a room with no air and perfect hinges? | It would swing for ever | It would stop at once | It would swing higher and higher | It would swing faster and faster
test: In Newton's cradle you swing two balls in. What happens? | Two balls fly out the other side | One ball flies out twice as fast | All the balls swing together | Nothing moves at all
test: you're at the top of the swing and your tummy goes weird. why | You stop for an instant and press less on the seat | Your stomach gets heavier at the top | The chains pull you upwards | The air presses on your tummy
test: Your grandfather clock runs slow. Which way do you move the weight? | Up, to make the pendulum shorter | Down, to make the pendulum longer | Off, to make it lighter | Nowhere, clean the face
test: A pendulum is made four times longer. What happens to its swing time? | It takes twice as long | It takes four times as long | It takes half as long | It stays exactly the same
test: In a cartoon a pendulum keeps swinging higher and higher with nobody pushing. Is that real? | No, it would swing lower and lower | Yes, pendulums always speed up as they go | Yes, if the room is warm | No, it would stop at once
test: Why must the area round a wrecking ball be cleared of people? | The ball can swing back towards the crane | The ball might float away | The noise is far too loud for people's ears to bear | The building pulls the ball in
test: More water in the singing glass. What happens to the note? | It gets lower | It gets higher | It stays the same | It stops completely
test: How is a roller coaster like a swing? | It swaps height and speed on every hill | It always goes back and forth | It has the same rhythm as a clock | It is pushed by a person
test: Which would swing back and forth the fastest? | A short swing in the toddlers' park | A long swing on a tall frame in the big park | A tall swing with a heavy child | A long rope swing over a river
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determinant of the period of a simple pendulum of modest amplitude? | Its length | The mass of its bob | The force of the initial push | The material of its cord
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a body of soldiers is instructed to break step upon crossing a bridge. | Regular impulses matching the natural rhythm of the bridge could increase its oscillation | The tread of soldiers wears away the surface of the bridge | Marching renders the soldiers heavier upon the bridge | The noise of marching disturbs the inhabitants nearby
exam: It is given that a child is seated upon a swing in motion. Ascertain the position at which the speed of the swing is greatest. | The lowest point of its path | The highest point of its path | A point midway towards the top | The speed is uniform throughout
exam: Determine the effect upon the period of a swing of the substitution of a heavier rider for a lighter rider. | The period remains unaltered | The period is considerably lengthened | The period is considerably shortened | The swing ceases to oscillate
